{
  "description": "issue #682 (slice I3): a handler block is NOT a procedure body, so `return`/`stop` inside it are `return`/`stop` OUTSIDE a procedure — even when the `when` was registered inside one. Here `when \"start\"` is registered inside `define setup`; the `\"start\"` handler fires immediately (the run has started) and hits `return 5`. Without a boundary conversion the `return` signal would escape into `setup`'s call frame and be silently consumed as `setup`'s own return; instead the handler boundary converts it to `ol-return-outside-proc` (spec/error-model.md), pointing at the `return` keyword. The `return` trace event (seq 7) is still emitted by the statement before the boundary reclassifies the escaping signal.",
